1
00:00:06,953 --> 00:00:08,194
[SPEAKER_01]: This topic of non-conflict.

2
00:00:09,336 --> 00:00:19,327
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just read a verse, two verses from the Dhammapada, chapter 10, violence, or it's translated as the stick.

3
00:00:22,147 --> 00:00:23,987
[SPEAKER_01]: What is malicious speech?

4
00:00:24,087 --> 00:00:26,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it the choice of words?

5
00:00:26,128 --> 00:00:28,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it the intonation?

6
00:00:28,548 --> 00:00:29,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it shouting at you or not?

7
00:00:50,575 --> 00:00:52,877
[SPEAKER_00]: What determines malicious speech as malicious speech?

8
00:00:53,157 --> 00:00:53,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intention.

9
00:00:54,538 --> 00:00:54,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

10
00:00:55,159 --> 00:00:55,780
[SPEAKER_00]: What intention?

11
00:00:56,460 --> 00:00:57,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention to harm.

12
00:00:58,822 --> 00:01:00,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the first point here.

13
00:01:00,684 --> 00:01:06,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Because many people are caught up in the idea that speech in itself is malicious.

14
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:12,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the words I've chosen means immediately means this is the motivation.

15
00:01:12,474 --> 00:01:15,597
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, the same works for any type of action.

16
00:01:17,182 --> 00:01:24,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Action is determined as good or bad, wholesome or wholesome, malicious or non-malicious, by the intention behind it.

17
00:01:25,245 --> 00:01:27,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by the content of it.

18
00:01:27,806 --> 00:01:36,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Certain type of action is always unwholesome, such as killing, sexual intercourse, and so on, in terms of the precepts, lying, stealing.

19
00:01:37,069 --> 00:01:37,829
[SPEAKER_00]: That's abandoned.

20
00:01:38,550 --> 00:01:40,850
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not to be done, as the Buddha said.

21
00:01:41,270 --> 00:01:48,633
[SPEAKER_00]: But every other action that's not covered by that, is then basically determined by your intention, and speech especially.

22
00:01:50,298 --> 00:01:54,824
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's important thing, again, to realize the motivation behind the action is what determines it.

23
00:01:54,864 --> 00:01:58,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, I will never use these words, I will never raise my voice.

24
00:02:00,049 --> 00:02:01,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I must not be malicious.

25
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:05,454
[SPEAKER_00]: That means you have a view that maliciousness is there where it isn't.

26
00:02:06,135 --> 00:02:09,037
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, you're not seeing it for where it actually is.

27
00:02:09,077 --> 00:02:11,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's going to continue and you maintain maliciousness.

28
00:02:11,559 --> 00:02:12,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

29
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:21,308
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can have your perfect, you know, psychotherapy type of speech to never say anything conflicting to anyone and hate their guts.

30
00:02:21,428 --> 00:02:21,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

31
00:02:22,910 --> 00:02:28,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Fully be rooted in ill-will, but have this perfect tone, soft words spoken to them.

32
00:02:28,731 --> 00:02:29,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, lovely smile on your face.

33
00:02:29,951 --> 00:02:31,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, lovely smile on your face, exactly.

34
00:02:31,852 --> 00:02:42,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is that, there's that sutta where the Buddha said that, said some companions in a holy life are softly spoken, agreeable, loved by everybody, but they have wrong views.

35
00:02:42,154 --> 00:02:49,695
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't understand what the root of the problem is, they don't understand the way out of it, they don't understand anything, but they're pleasing to everybody, and they're extremely blameworthy.

36
00:02:51,210 --> 00:02:52,892
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because the view made them blameworthy.

37
00:02:53,372 --> 00:02:57,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you have the other type of people, who are actually upsetting to others.

38
00:02:57,755 --> 00:03:05,201
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't have agreeable speech to others, but they also have the right view, and they know where the problem is, and they can basically teach that, and so on.

39
00:03:05,761 --> 00:03:07,122
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, like, they're not blameworthy.

40
00:03:08,223 --> 00:03:10,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they cannot have a malicious intent.

41
00:03:11,025 --> 00:03:11,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they understood it.

42
00:03:12,006 --> 00:03:13,567
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter how you speak.

43
00:03:16,029 --> 00:03:17,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Speech is not malicious by itself.

44
00:03:17,651 --> 00:03:18,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

45
00:03:18,031 --> 00:03:20,633
[SPEAKER_00]: It's why you're saying it, and what's your intention behind.

46
00:03:23,289 --> 00:03:25,112
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, that's a... sometimes...

47
00:03:26,194 --> 00:03:31,884
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how often, but... but I've seen a number of times that people refuse to even accept.

48
00:03:32,851 --> 00:03:37,115
[SPEAKER_00]: This, which is very clear, and all you need to do is read more suttas, and you'll see it's said many times.

49
00:03:37,735 --> 00:03:58,353
[SPEAKER_00]: But people still refuse to accept that, because, well, if you accept that the motivation determines whether your speech is good or bad, whether your action is good or bad, that means then you become more aware of your motivation and on your responsibility in regard to things, as opposed to just blindly adhere to not raising your voice, not saying certain words,

50
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:02,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Always speaking with certain facial expression, and that's your practice.

51
00:04:03,433 --> 00:04:05,775
[SPEAKER_00]: That's palpable, that's doable, that's observable.

52
00:04:06,556 --> 00:04:08,257
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's nothing to do with understanding.

53
00:04:09,238 --> 00:04:12,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, yeah, it means it's also presentable.

54
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:13,521
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very presentable, yeah.

55
00:04:13,781 --> 00:04:14,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's very relatable.

56
00:04:14,582 --> 00:04:15,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will praise.

57
00:04:15,642 --> 00:04:16,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Very relatable externally.

58
00:04:17,224 --> 00:04:19,005
[SPEAKER_01]: A good report has been spread around.

59
00:04:19,085 --> 00:04:19,706
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fine.

60
00:04:19,806 --> 00:04:21,267
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a natural thing to assume that.

61
00:04:21,287 --> 00:04:27,712
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, you have to ask yourself, okay, but am I in position to know, like, what the way out of suffering is, and how this contributes to it or not?

62
00:04:28,292 --> 00:04:31,954
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is no, you mustn't take for granted any assumption that you have.

63
00:04:32,294 --> 00:04:41,097
[SPEAKER_00]: I know the first time I read that sutta, where that monk was basically really rude to all these people and other monks, talking down to them and calling them names and whatnot.

64
00:04:41,878 --> 00:04:43,738
[SPEAKER_00]: And people got upset and went to the Buddha.

65
00:04:44,559 --> 00:04:48,240
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, obviously, it must have been a big deal, because many people went to the Buddha for that sake.

66
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:49,741
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't just one person complaining.

67
00:04:50,961 --> 00:04:54,403
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha called him and then, oh, so he was an arahant.

68
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:58,642
[SPEAKER_00]: And he didn't tell him, but you should still police your speech and change your habits.

69
00:04:58,742 --> 00:05:00,063
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he said, no, he's an arahant.

70
00:05:00,143 --> 00:05:00,664
[SPEAKER_01]: He's blameless.

71
00:05:00,904 --> 00:05:02,885
[SPEAKER_00]: He's blameless, doesn't matter how he speaks.

72
00:05:02,965 --> 00:05:06,767
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he said, the reason he still speaks like that, because of his previous lives and so on and so forth.

73
00:05:07,428 --> 00:05:11,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And at the time when I read that, I... well, I couldn't make sense out of it.

74
00:05:12,211 --> 00:05:16,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in my mind, the idea of an arahant is just this always, like, you cannot say...

75
00:05:16,974 --> 00:05:17,795
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, well, wait a minute.

76
00:05:18,635 --> 00:05:20,736
[SPEAKER_00]: If something is upsetting to somebody, it doesn't mean

77
00:05:23,608 --> 00:05:26,549
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the intention of the person who does it, that's what determines it.

78
00:05:27,329 --> 00:05:35,252
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you realize, oh, so then I should start looking at my own intentionality behind my own seemingly justifiable actions and choices I'm making.

79
00:05:36,212 --> 00:05:42,134
[SPEAKER_00]: And that already makes you doubtful, fills you with anxiety, because you realize you can tomorrow decide to do the otherwise.

80
00:05:42,234 --> 00:05:43,315
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just walk away.

81
00:05:44,368 --> 00:05:46,930
[SPEAKER_00]: You are not justified to do anything unless you choose to do it.

82
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:49,372
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize that's your only justification.

83
00:05:49,552 --> 00:05:50,793
[SPEAKER_00]: That I've chosen it.

84
00:05:50,853 --> 00:05:51,194
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

85
00:05:51,814 --> 00:05:53,155
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not a justification.

86
00:05:53,195 --> 00:05:56,878
[SPEAKER_00]: That's felt much more responsibly, much more heavily, much more unpleasantly.

87
00:05:57,399 --> 00:06:10,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's better then for me to ignore, maybe, the suttas that talk about that, and then just focus and blindly adhere to certain types of behavior, certain type of speech, certain type of soft voice, certain type of this, certain type of that.

88
00:06:11,507 --> 00:06:14,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you naturally do that and speak like that, that's absolutely fine.

89
00:06:16,566 --> 00:06:24,788
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're doing it, so basically, you think that's what the practice is, which then allows you to not look at the motivation why you're doing it, that's why it's not fine.

90
00:06:24,808 --> 00:06:35,572
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might be doing it to provide motivation, but you must realize that speaking harshly will probably result in retaliation from the other person.

91
00:06:35,832 --> 00:06:36,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course.

92
00:06:36,432 --> 00:06:36,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

93
00:06:36,812 --> 00:06:38,493
[SPEAKER_00]: You have that perspective.

94
00:06:38,933 --> 00:06:40,633
[SPEAKER_00]: It's part of the deal.

95
00:06:40,713 --> 00:06:41,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

96
00:06:41,133 --> 00:06:46,235
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot pretend or say like that monk... and that monk did not complain about people complaining about him.

97
00:06:47,838 --> 00:06:52,281
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure, him being an arahant, he understood that, well, it's part of the package.

98
00:06:53,661 --> 00:07:01,286
[SPEAKER_00]: If he were to, by any chance, decide to deal with all these complaints, he would have maybe changed his speech, but he would have had a practical reason for it.

99
00:07:01,766 --> 00:07:07,049
[SPEAKER_00]: He would have not changed it out of some misguided view that, softly speaking, means the dhamma.

100
00:07:08,325 --> 00:07:10,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Not raising your voice means the dhamma.

101
00:07:11,387 --> 00:07:12,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't.

102
00:07:12,947 --> 00:07:17,550
[SPEAKER_00]: And all you need to do is read the Vinaya Suttas and see how the Buddha spoke to the monks that broke the rules.

103
00:07:18,210 --> 00:07:20,952
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember there was another thing I was quite shocked when I saw it first time.

104
00:07:21,212 --> 00:07:29,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in your mind, the Buddha is this mix of, you know, Mahayana and everything else, this loving kindness, you know, joy always on his face.

105
00:07:30,116 --> 00:07:34,899
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, I came across the Vinaya Suttas when the monks done things they shouldn't have.

106
00:07:35,541 --> 00:07:51,172
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha came and rebuked them, and the words he used to them, you're like... Well, if you don't... if you wouldn't know that, eh... where the speech is full of ill-will, is determined by the motivation behind the speech, not the content of the speech, you would think the Buddha was furious.

107
00:07:52,976 --> 00:07:55,657
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I don't want to repeat stuff he said, because it was quite...

108
00:07:55,697 --> 00:07:57,417
[SPEAKER_01]: It was like, you're stupid, you fool...

109
00:07:57,437 --> 00:08:04,699
[SPEAKER_00]: He was worse than that, when it came to those sexual rules that monks broke, the words he used in comparisons he made.

110
00:08:04,899 --> 00:08:13,121
[SPEAKER_00]: It was, like, shocking at the time, but then you realize, well, actually, that was the appropriate, proportional response to the action they committed.

111
00:08:13,701 --> 00:08:19,282
[SPEAKER_00]: If he was like, it's all fine, brother, next time you'll do better, then I'm going to take it seriously, exactly.

112
00:08:19,802 --> 00:08:22,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole order will decline as a result of it.

113
00:08:23,363 --> 00:08:28,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if he was that level of, you know, harshness, means that's how much he was required.

114
00:08:29,645 --> 00:08:37,367
[SPEAKER_00]: But you to judge that, that he was full of ill-will because of the words he's used, well, means your own ideas of it are quite misguided, where the problem is.

115
00:08:42,949 --> 00:08:49,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of, you know, having that perspective that

116
00:08:52,905 --> 00:09:07,218
[SPEAKER_01]: There is going to be some sort of retaliation, that a relationship means that there's going to be some sort of... all relationships will be conflictive, conflicting, so...

117
00:09:07,258 --> 00:09:07,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Conflicting, yeah.

118
00:09:08,078 --> 00:09:11,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think that's every relationship already is, to some degree.

119
00:09:11,962 --> 00:09:14,084
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always a conflict when two beings come together.

120
00:09:15,125 --> 00:09:18,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Conflict in a sense being, you know, they're already different in some regard.

121
00:09:20,458 --> 00:09:29,923
[SPEAKER_00]: But if, say, if both of the people, beings, were to protect their mindset of non-conflict, non-ill-will, there cannot be any conflict arising.

122
00:09:29,943 --> 00:09:31,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what words they use.

123
00:09:33,825 --> 00:09:45,330
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are unable to get full of ill-will towards me, even if I'm full of ill-will towards you, that means already there is no room for ill-will to arise in our relationship.

124
00:09:46,391 --> 00:09:49,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it needs to be reciprocated, needs to be given back.

125
00:09:51,891 --> 00:09:55,573
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, imagine if both of us are incapable of ill-will towards each other.

126
00:09:56,354 --> 00:09:59,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because I love you so much, and you're a dear friend of mine.

127
00:09:59,976 --> 00:10:04,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, no, I protect my mind of non-ill-will so much, that I don't care who's in front of me.

128
00:10:05,260 --> 00:10:07,241
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not allow my mind to hate them.

129
00:10:07,801 --> 00:10:10,363
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you do the same, that is the divine abode.

130
00:10:10,583 --> 00:10:14,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Incapable of hatred on both sides, both parties.

131
00:10:14,986 --> 00:10:17,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of what's said, what's done, what's perceived.

132
00:10:18,374 --> 00:10:19,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, truly harmonious.

133
00:10:20,035 --> 00:10:26,156
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean I'll be oblivious to your behavior, because I'm so full of non-ill-will, that you can do anything.

134
00:10:26,637 --> 00:10:28,657
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I'll still recognize that was a foolish thing to do.

135
00:10:29,037 --> 00:10:33,199
[SPEAKER_00]: And now I can tell you, because it won't be rooted in my hatred of you, nor ill-will.

136
00:10:33,219 --> 00:10:40,201
[SPEAKER_00]: And furthermore, you'll be able to hear it, and not lose your composure, because you will not have ill-will towards me.

137
00:10:41,061 --> 00:10:44,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter how blunt things can get,

138
00:10:45,152 --> 00:10:46,273
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not lose the context.

139
00:10:47,194 --> 00:10:48,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither party will lose the context.

140
00:10:48,936 --> 00:11:02,431
[SPEAKER_01]: But the party, the person who has that non-conflicting mindset, that right intentions, who's coming from the right place, if he says, well, you're a fool,

141
00:11:03,512 --> 00:11:08,393
[SPEAKER_01]: But he must also realize if that person is kind of... will be able to hear it or not.

142
00:11:09,174 --> 00:11:11,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Because usually people are not going to be able to hear that.

143
00:11:11,494 --> 00:11:11,554
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

144
00:11:11,574 --> 00:11:13,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they already don't have that context to begin with.

145
00:11:14,115 --> 00:11:26,658
[SPEAKER_00]: And they will never have that context to begin with if they don't start looking for it at the right place, which is your own mindset in regard to how you're feeling, regardless of what you heard, seen, taste, touched and so on.

146
00:11:26,678 --> 00:11:29,379
[SPEAKER_01]: The wise person knows when to say... knows...

147
00:11:29,799 --> 00:11:30,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

148
00:11:30,660 --> 00:11:31,860
[SPEAKER_01]: When is the right time to say.

149
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:33,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Where it's not... where it's going to be beneficial.

150
00:11:34,081 --> 00:11:35,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

151
00:11:35,322 --> 00:11:35,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

152
00:11:35,442 --> 00:11:40,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they already have that greater perspective of non-conflict, non-ill-will, non-resistance to...

153
00:11:40,545 --> 00:11:47,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, if you don't resist to how you're feeling, you're not gonna become impatient to say something.

154
00:11:48,050 --> 00:11:56,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the reason people become impatient and say things, right things, but at the wrong time, is because they can't bear how they're feeling at the time, so they must say it quickly.

155
00:11:57,815 --> 00:12:04,999
[SPEAKER_00]: But if your priorities don't resist how you feel, regardless of what it is, you cannot even give in to that.

156
00:12:05,379 --> 00:12:09,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, whenever you say it, it would be the right time, because you're not saying it out of impatience.

157
00:12:10,542 --> 00:12:18,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is a form of resisting to how you're feeling at the time.

158
00:12:18,466 --> 00:12:22,569
[SPEAKER_00]: And you also will not be bothered by the thought, what if I never say this?

159
00:12:23,109 --> 00:12:24,970
[SPEAKER_00]: What if this person never hears that?

160
00:12:25,943 --> 00:12:26,763
[SPEAKER_00]: You're indifferent to it.

161
00:12:26,844 --> 00:12:27,664
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not your problem.

162
00:12:28,765 --> 00:12:29,645
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what your duty is.

163
00:12:29,685 --> 00:12:30,926
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you know you can say it.

164
00:12:32,046 --> 00:12:36,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you won't be rooted in any form of resistance or impatience that you might have.

165
00:12:39,410 --> 00:12:42,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yes, literally, what if I were to die after the next in-breath?

166
00:12:43,713 --> 00:12:47,135
[SPEAKER_00]: How important would it be to me to say this?

167
00:12:47,155 --> 00:12:55,219
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize the only reason why things are important, pressing, I must say it, you cannot resist, compulsive, is because you resist how you're feeling and you want to quickly deal with that.

168
00:12:56,873 --> 00:12:58,275
[SPEAKER_01]: You, yeah, you're patient.

169
00:12:58,395 --> 00:13:02,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you forsake the framework for the sake of dealing with the content.

170
00:13:06,468 --> 00:13:09,933
[SPEAKER_01]: And the next verse following, eh...

171
00:13:10,948 --> 00:13:11,408
[SPEAKER_00]: What was that?

172
00:13:11,468 --> 00:13:12,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Was it the broken gong?

173
00:13:12,749 --> 00:13:13,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

174
00:13:13,530 --> 00:13:15,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what I was saying, basically.

175
00:13:15,332 --> 00:13:15,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

176
00:13:15,972 --> 00:13:23,638
[SPEAKER_00]: When, even if I'm fully of ill-will and your mind is incapable of ill-will, because it's not reciprocated back to me, there is no ill-will in the relation.

177
00:13:23,698 --> 00:13:25,600
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like, the gong is broken.

178
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:30,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how much I'm hitting it, there will be no sound resonating, because it needs the response.

179
00:13:31,104 --> 00:13:31,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

180
00:13:31,825 --> 00:13:33,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, imagine if both sides are broken.

181
00:13:34,027 --> 00:13:38,250
[SPEAKER_00]: The stick you're hitting the gong is broken, there is no resonance of any ill-will.

182
00:13:39,011 --> 00:13:40,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible to occur.

183
00:13:44,479 --> 00:13:48,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that conflict that's coming from one can only go so far.

184
00:13:49,020 --> 00:13:49,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

185
00:13:49,740 --> 00:13:50,741
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the dhamma part.

186
00:13:50,781 --> 00:13:52,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Hatred breeds hate.

187
00:13:52,441 --> 00:13:52,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

188
00:13:52,841 --> 00:13:54,101
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like one of the...

189
00:13:54,161 --> 00:13:54,762
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs.

190
00:13:54,822 --> 00:13:55,162
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs.

191
00:13:55,182 --> 00:13:56,782
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot occur otherwise.

192
00:13:58,843 --> 00:14:01,483
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is going to be hatred.

193
00:14:01,503 --> 00:14:06,865
[SPEAKER_00]: There is going to be... Can you have hatred without resistance to what you hate?

194
00:14:08,445 --> 00:14:11,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any form of hatred other than resistance?

195
00:14:11,246 --> 00:14:12,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Amplified resistance?

196
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:13,800
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't.

197
00:14:14,220 --> 00:14:21,404
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're incapable of resistance towards how you're feeling, would you be able of hating anything based on your feeling?

198
00:14:21,564 --> 00:14:22,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

199
00:14:23,345 --> 00:14:25,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of trying to deal with your hatred, chase it.

200
00:14:25,385 --> 00:14:28,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Just stop resisting how you're feeling in regard to anything and everyone.

201
00:14:30,528 --> 00:14:31,848
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be incapable of hatred.

202
00:14:32,048 --> 00:14:35,070
[SPEAKER_01]: In the world, there will always be those who hate.

203
00:14:35,450 --> 00:14:36,931
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the world is.

204
00:14:36,951 --> 00:14:38,872
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you expect?

205
00:14:39,332 --> 00:14:41,753
[SPEAKER_00]: You expect for you to protect your mind of non-hate.

206
00:14:42,977 --> 00:14:44,198
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's everybody hating.

207
00:14:44,678 --> 00:14:54,722
[SPEAKER_01]: But trying to make others not hate and going out of your way to make others not hate... That will be rooted in you resisting how you feel on account of experiencing hate.

208
00:14:55,182 --> 00:14:57,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So... You're missing the point.

209
00:14:57,243 --> 00:14:59,644
[SPEAKER_00]: You will make yourself liable to yourself hating, then.

210
00:15:00,425 --> 00:15:01,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you abandon the framework.

211
00:15:01,305 --> 00:15:03,826
[SPEAKER_01]: You're setting yourself up for a major task that will never be completed.

212
00:15:04,086 --> 00:15:04,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible to complete.

213
00:15:04,987 --> 00:15:09,509
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a satisfaction all the way.

214
00:15:09,629 --> 00:15:10,809
[SPEAKER_01]: It's futile, completely.

215
00:15:11,269 --> 00:15:12,410
[SPEAKER_01]: The opposite of the peaceful

216
00:15:19,150 --> 00:15:20,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to make a difference.

217
00:15:21,611 --> 00:15:21,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

218
00:15:22,731 --> 00:15:23,452
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

219
00:15:23,492 --> 00:15:26,492
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will know that if you look where your motivation is rooted.

220
00:15:27,093 --> 00:15:30,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Even motivation, like, why am I practicing in such and such manner?

221
00:15:30,914 --> 00:15:31,354
[SPEAKER_00]: What for?

222
00:15:32,594 --> 00:15:35,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it to manage what I don't want to accept on the level of my feeling?

223
00:15:36,435 --> 00:15:38,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it to develop indifference towards my feelings?

224
00:15:38,976 --> 00:15:39,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it this?

225
00:15:39,556 --> 00:15:40,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it that?

226
00:15:41,017 --> 00:15:45,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And if your practice is about, you know, dealing with hate, dealing with this, spreading love,

227
00:15:47,020 --> 00:15:49,502
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, well, that's quite a limited application already.

228
00:15:49,963 --> 00:15:58,871
[SPEAKER_00]: It's dependent on these things, which means you cannot possibly prevent people from acting foolishly, saying things that would be upsetting, and it's even possible.

229
00:15:59,551 --> 00:16:04,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the only reason you would be trying to prevent that is because you still hold the view that that will basically save you from the discomfort.

230
00:16:06,858 --> 00:16:09,200
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as such, you remain liable to the same discomfort.

231
00:16:11,038 --> 00:16:12,819
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that verse again I'll read.

232
00:16:13,639 --> 00:16:23,782
[SPEAKER_01]: If, like a broken gong, you silence yourself, you have approached nibbāna, for malice or contention is no longer found in you.

233
00:16:23,802 --> 00:16:24,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's it.

234
00:16:25,082 --> 00:16:30,804
[SPEAKER_00]: What do malice, contention, hatred, ill-will have in common?

235
00:16:30,824 --> 00:16:31,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Resistance.

236
00:16:31,404 --> 00:16:32,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Resistance, yeah.

237
00:16:32,685 --> 00:16:36,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you resist something if that thing is not felt?

238
00:16:36,906 --> 00:16:37,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

239
00:16:40,907 --> 00:16:47,793
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, if you stop resisting to how you feel, you then stop cultivating malice, hatred, contentions, everything else.

240
00:16:48,054 --> 00:16:49,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible to engage with those things.

241
00:16:51,337 --> 00:16:51,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, silence.

242
00:16:52,377 --> 00:16:52,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

243
00:16:52,898 --> 00:16:53,779
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the true silence.

244
00:16:53,799 --> 00:16:54,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the noble silence.

245
00:16:54,559 --> 00:16:57,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, you know, slowing your mouth shut.

246
00:16:57,822 --> 00:16:58,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

247
00:16:58,663 --> 00:16:59,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Noble silence.

248
00:16:59,604 --> 00:17:00,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Noble silence, yeah.

